// The packet schedule: apps/rtq/rtq.bonsai run the way Embree's
// BVHNIntersectorKHybrid<8, 16> runs a packet of sixteen rays -- the schedule
// the comparison against rtcIntersect16 and rtcOccluded16 is made with
// (compare.sh --schedule packet16, which has the driver answer Embree's side
// in packets of sixteen too, the same sixteen consecutive rays a packet on
// both sides). Compiled beside the program as a second input:
//
//     ./build/compiler -p ssa --no-heap --ffp-contract \
//         -i apps/rtq/rtq.bonsai -i apps/rtq/schedules/packet16.bonsai ...
//
// The tree and its bytes are embree.bonsai's, the eight-wide tree
// (trees/bvh8.bonsai) in Embree's bytes (layouts/embree8.bonsai); what
// differs is which loop is the gang. embree.bonsai vectorizes the loops of
// the traversal -- a node's eight children tested at once, a leaf's four
// triangles at once, one ray at a time. This file vectorizes the loop over
// the rays instead, and leaves the traversal's loops as loops: sixteen rays
// make a gang, the machine's AVX-512 register of 32-bit lanes and Embree's
// K for rtcIntersect16, and the traversal is made by the gang as a whole --
// one node the lanes share, a mask of the lanes whose ray reached it, one
// stack for the gang -- which is a packet traversal (Wald, Slusallek,
// Benthin & Wagner, "Interactive Rendering with Coherent Ray Tracing",
// Eurographics 2001) written as the composition of the directives below,
// in this order: the gang first, then the sort, then the stack (see
// apps/pbrt/schedules/packet.bonsai and tests/bonsai/ssa/
// vectorize-packet-traversal.bonsai for the same composition over a binary
// tree). Embree's packet kernel over this tree is BVHNIntersectorKHybrid
// (kernels/bvh/bvh_intersector_hybrid.cpp, `intersect` and `occluded`),
// audited step for step against this file in PLAN.md ("Ray packets against
// rtcIntersect16"); what each directive answers of it is noted beside it,
// and what no directive answers is noted at the end.
import apps/rtq/schedules/layouts/embree8;

schedule {
    // Embree: K = 16, a packet of sixteen rays through rtcIntersect16. The
    // batch's parfor over the rays in gangs of sixteen, the machine's
    // vector width in 32-bit lanes; `n` is a runtime count, and vectorize
    // wants a constant gang width, so split peels a width-sixteen inner
    // loop off it first and that is what is vectorized. The `true` asks for
    // the split's tail: a batch whose count is not a multiple of sixteen
    // runs its last gang partly full, the guard the tail puts in front of
    // the body being that gang's mask -- as the driver turns off the last
    // lanes of Embree's last packet (its `valid` ints). The gang is made
    // before the traversals below are sorted and put on their stacks, and
    // the order is the point (see the top of the file).
    trace_all.split(i, i_gang, i_lane, 16, true)
             .vectorize(i_lane);
    occluded_all.split(i, i_gang, i_lane, 16, true)
                .vectorize(i_lane);

    // Embree: the chunk loop of BVHNIntersectorKHybrid::intersect. At a
    // node, each child in stored order is tested against the sixteen rays
    // at once (BVHNNodeIntersectorK::intersect: the child's six bounds
    // broadcast against the rays' reciprocal directions, `intersectNodeK`),
    // a child no ray hits is passed over, and of the children hit the one
    // the rays enter nearest is descended into and the rest wait on the
    // stack -- the same traversal as the single ray's, over a gang: the
    // key is each lane's own `distmin`, the slab test's entry distance,
    // `inf` where the lane's ray misses the child (Embree's
    // `select(lhit, lnearP, inf)`), and the order is settled once for the
    // gang by a vote of the lanes on each comparison -- the majority of
    // the lanes that are on, the user's choice of 2026-10-08
    // (Instruction::Op::Vote, SSA/SortRecursion.cpp) -- since one child is
    // descended into for the whole gang. The loop over the children is NOT
    // vectorized here: the lanes are the rays, and a node's children are
    // tested one at a time against all of them, as Embree's chunk loop does
    // (`for (unsigned i = 0; i < N; i++)`), not eight at a time against
    // one ray as embree.bonsai does.
    //
    // Embree: stack_node[stackSizeChunk] with stackSizeChunk = 1 + (N - 1) *
    // maxDepth = 561 for N = 8 and maxDepth 80 (bvh_intersector_hybrid.h;
    // no +3 here: the chunk stack is not written sixteen wide) -- the most
    // a packet's traversal can have waiting. Loopify puts the gang's
    // recursion on one stack of that depth: a stack of node references and
    // one of lane masks (SSA/QueueRecursion.h), where Embree keeps a stack
    // of node references and one of the sixteen rays' entry distances
    // (`stack_near`), from which it re-derives the active lanes at each pop
    // (`curDist < tray.tfar`).
    //
    // Embree: TriangleMIntersectorKMoeller<4, 16>::intersect -- at a leaf,
    // each triangle of a Triangle4 block in turn against the sixteen rays
    // (`for (size_t i = 0; i < M; i++) { if (!tri.valid(i)) break; ...
    // intersectK }`), the block's slot broadcast and the rays the lanes. The
    // loop over a leaf's triangles is left a loop here too, each triangle
    // tested by the gang; the epilog's masked update of each lane's best is
    // the argmin's own.
    trace.sort(triangles.Interior,
               |i : u32, r : Ray, lo : vector[vec3f, 8], hi : vector[vec3f, 8]|
               distmin(r, AABB{lo[i], hi[i]}))
         .loopify(561);
    // Embree: MoellerTrumboreIntersectorK::intersectK -- `if (likely(none
    // (valid))) return false` after each of the three edge tests and the
    // depth test: in a gang, a test of whether any lane goes on in front of
    // what follows each `if` of intersectsp_ray_tri (SSA/Linearize.h,
    // "Which arms get a gadget"), which is what the directive puts there
    // (`skip`). The same directive as embree.bonsai's, for a different
    // reason: there it is the single ray's `early_out` over the block's four
    // lanes; here it is the packet's over the sixteen rays.
    trace.skip(intersectsp_ray_tri);
    // Embree: no prefetch in the chunk loop -- BVH::prefetch is called in
    // the coherent (frustum) path and in the single-ray traversal only --
    // so none is scheduled here.

    // Embree: the chunk loop of BVHNIntersectorKHybrid::occluded -- the
    // same test of each child against the sixteen rays, the children hit
    // taken in their stored order with no sort (`cur = child` for each hit
    // child, the one before it pushed: the last hit child is descended into
    // and the earlier ones wait, as the single ray's traverseAnyHit orders
    // them), a lane that finds a hit turned off (`terminated`, its tfar set
    // to -inf) and the traversal ended when every lane is (`if (all
    // (terminated)) break`), which is the gang's `any`: one bool per lane,
    // settled only when every lane's is (SSA/QueueRecursion.h). The leaf is
    // TriangleMIntersectorKMoeller::occluded, the same per-triangle test of
    // the sixteen rays with the same `none(valid)` exits.
    occluded.loopify(561);
    occluded.skip(intersectsp_ray_tri);

    // The closest-point query as embree.bonsai runs it, one point at a
    // time with the node test eight wide: Embree has no packet form of
    // rtcPointQuery (rtcPointQuery4/8/16 are scalar loops over it,
    // kernels/common/rtcore.cpp), so there is no packet comparison to make,
    // and the driver's closest-point rows under this schedule are the
    // scalar query's (benchmark.sh does not run them for a packet
    // schedule).
    closest.sort(triangles.Interior,
                 |i : u32, q : Point, lo : vector[vec3f, 8], hi : vector[vec3f, 8]|
                 distmin(q, AABB{lo[i], hi[i]}))
           .vectorize(triangles.Interior.children)
           .loopify(564);
    closest.prefetch(triangles.Interior.children, 4);

    // What Embree's kernel does that no directive here says -- the steps
    // the comparison does not match, recorded so the table is read right
    // (PLAN.md has the audit):
    //
    // 1. It is a hybrid (Benthin, Wald, Woop, Ernst & Mark, "Combining
    //    Single and Packet-Ray Tracing for Arbitrary Ray Distributions on
    //    the Intel MIC Architecture", TVCG 2012): at each pop, and again
    //    after a node's children are tested, if at most switchThreshold of
    //    the sixteen rays are still active -- 14 for K = 16 (7 for K = 8
    //    over an eight-wide tree, 3 for K = 4) -- it pushes the node back
    //    and traverses each active ray alone from it, with rtcIntersect1's
    //    own kernel (intersect1: the eight-wide node test of one ray, its
    //    sorted descent, its stack of 564, its prefetch, the four-wide
    //    Triangle4 test of that one ray), then continues popping with the
    //    packet. The schedule here traverses the gang as a packet to the
    //    end.
    // 2. Before traversing, it partitions the packet by direction octant
    //    (`ray.octant()`, the three sign bits): where the packet holds rays
    //    of opposing signs it is traversed as sub-packets, each the rays
    //    within one sign bit of its first ray's octant, one after another
    //    over their own stack. The gang here is the sixteen rays as given.
    // 3. Its order of the hit children is a scan with lane-wise any-compares
    //    -- the child kept to descend into is replaced when `any(childDist <
    //    curDist)`, the others pushed as met, then the top two or three of
    //    the stack swapped by `any(near[-2] < near[-1])` and its kin -- not
    //    a full sort; here the majority of the lanes decides each comparison
    //    of a full sorting network, by the user's decision (2026-10-08: the
    //    majority vote for the vectorized sort), so the gang's order is the
    //    order most of its rays want, where Embree's is the first order any
    //    ray wants.
    // The directives to say 1 and 2 do not exist, and adding a scheduling
    // construct is the user's call; 3 is decided.
}
